WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.834
Buy-sell agreements.

2
00:00:01.893 --> 00:00:06.037
How life insurance keeps a business running smoothly when an owner dies.

3
00:00:06.983 --> 00:00:08.263
When a business owner dies,

4
00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:11.196
their share of the business usually passes to their heirs.

5
00:00:11.488 --> 00:00:15.566
Without a plan, the surviving owners could end up running the company with a spouse

6
00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:17.419
or child who never worked there.

7
00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:20.113
A buy-sell agreement solves that.

8
00:00:20.915 --> 00:00:24.436
It's a legal agreement that the surviving owners will buy the deceased owner's

9
00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:27.938
share, and life insurance provides the money to do it.

10
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:33.673
In a cross-purchase plan, each owner buys a policy on every other owner,

11
00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:36.726
and is the owner and beneficiary of those policies.

12
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.699
With three owners, that's six policies.

13
00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:46.072
The formula is n times n minus one, so four owners would need twelve policies.

14
00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:50.240
In an entity plan, also called a stock redemption plan,

15
00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:53.432
the business itself buys one policy on each owner.

16
00:00:53.979 --> 00:00:56.091
Three owners, three policies.

17
00:00:56.452 --> 00:01:00.457
The business is the owner, the premium payer and the beneficiary,

18
00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:04.950
and it uses the death benefit to buy back the deceased owner's shares.

19
00:01:05.879 --> 00:01:09.709
To recap: in a cross-purchase plan, the owners insure each other.

20
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:13.796
In an entity plan, the business insures each owner.

21
00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:17.151
Either way, the premiums aren't tax-deductible,

22
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.843
and the death benefit is generally received income-tax free.
